WEBVTT

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:27.320
Years ago, I remember my schoolteacher announcing one day that there was an object so small, so tiny, we'd never be able to see it.

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:31.960
Well, fortunately, that didn't put me off.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:39.000
In fact, I've spent my entire career studying this amazing object without ever setting eyes on it.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:45.000
That elusive, remarkable object was the atom.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:50.160
Atoms are the fundamental building blocks of everything that we can see.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:54.920
So if you understand the atom, you understand the universe.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:01:00.080
The scientific quest to understand the atom is largely completed.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:08.600
But today, I believe we are entering a new era, an era where we do more than simply understand atoms, we begin to control them.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:13.640
Amazingly, we can now manipulate individual atoms.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:19.600
We can pick them up, move them around, and even play with them.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:40.400
All my life, I've worked with the theories of Einstein, Stephen Hawking, string theory, and now, for the first time, I'm actually seeing what I'm manipulating with my equations.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:46.080
You know, every time I move my body I move trillions of atoms.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:51.320
But to see a single atom move like this...

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:51.800
It's...

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.600
It's quite intoxicating.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:02:00.600
Today we can manipulate individual atoms, but this is just the beginning of a journey.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:10.840
A journey which will ultimately give us the power to manipulate the very stuff of our universe - matter itself.

00:02:09.919 --> 00:02:19.800
We are on the brink of a revolution which will give us control, exquisite control, of our physical world.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:29.080
In this programme I will show how our mastery of matter will profoundly change our lives and the world around us.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:34.120
How atomic-scale machines will revolutionise medicine.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:39.200
How invisibility and teleportation could make science fiction come true.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:42.440
How we will generate limitless clean energy.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:48.560
And how cheap space travel might give us all a new perspective on our planet.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:03:01.120
These technologies will give us the power to utterly transform our future.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:11.400
But how are we going to use this power?

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:26.440
Our deepest insight into the atomic world comes from the quantum theory.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:35.360
But the quantum theory gives us more than the ability just to manipulate individual atoms, it also underlies the architecture of the 20th century.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:43.720
The marvels of science, like the internet, laser beams, telecommunication satellites, radio, television, microwaves.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:48.520
Even the structure of the DNA molecule and biotechnology.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:53.760
All of it ultimately comes from the quantum theory.

00:03:52.960 --> 00:04:01.400
This theory offers a very different explanation of our world, one where the laws of conventional physics simply don't apply.

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:09.400
The quantum theory is so strange and bizarre, that even Einstein couldn't get his head around it.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:15.040
At the atomic level, we see a phenomenon that normally we would consider sheer madness.

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:19.880
In the real world, you can't simply disappear and reappear someplace else.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:20.800
That's nonsense.

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:26.200
But in the quantum world, it happens all the time.

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:39.200
At the level of atoms and subatomic particles, matter can be in an infinite number of places at once.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:49.840
The theory sounds preposterous, but it has one tiny thing going for it, and that is, it works.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:56.640
And in the coming century, it will enable us to radically transform our world.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:05:03.560
These Maglev trains travel on air due to magnetism, hovering just above the track.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:18.880
They cruise at up to 580 kilometres an hour, and they use less energy than regular trains, thanks to a quantum phenomenon - superconductivity.

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:28.720
Superconductors are a miracle of quantum physics, and they're an outstanding example of how we are gradually becoming masters of matter itself.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:34.000
Now, usually, superconductivity is quite difficult to achieve in the laboratory, and it's quite expensive.

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:51.360
But about 20 years ago, there was a revolution, when it was discovered that certain ceramics, like yttrium, barium, copper oxide here, become superconducting when exposed to ordinary liquid nitrogen.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:56.600
Now, liquid nitrogen costs as much as ordinary milk.

00:05:55.640 --> 00:06:04.520
By cooling the ceramic to -200 degrees centigrade, I'm effectively creating a new state of matter.

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:18.080
The new material loses all its electrical resistance and becomes superconducting, able to induce powerful magnetic fields.

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:24.800
I'm now going to place a magnet directly on top of the superconducting ceramic.

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:27.320
The polarity has to be just right.

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:29.040
There.

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:29.720
It's floating now.

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:36.480
This is the Meissner effect.

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:51.280
The presence of the magnet on top induces a secondary magnetic field within the superconductor, and the two magnetic fields repel each other, giving you the illusion of anti-gravity.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:58.680
Notice that this thing is spinning without any friction whatsoever.

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:07.960
Scientists hope to raise the temperature of the Meissner effect to avoid having to cool the ceramic.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:12.160
Imagine roads made out of superconductors at room temperature.

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:19.040
Cars would hover right over the road without even touching it, without using any gasoline whatsoever.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:24.920
Or, imagine a belt made out of this material.

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:25.080
You could then float in the air.

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:34.480
You could then fly almost like Superman, floating on magnetism.

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:50.120
So this is a realisation of the ancient dream of floating against gravity, anti-gravity, using magnetism to counteract the force of gravitation.

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:05.560
We won't just change transport with our mastery over matter.

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:13.720
In the future we'll also create materials with amazing new properties not found in nature.

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:21.720
Until recently there was a property of matter so fantastic, it was thought to exist only in myths and legends.

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:24.840
It was thought to be incompatible with the laws of physics.

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:31.800
But last year, scientists made it happen.

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:35.559
They created a material which shouldn't exist in nature.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:50.160
When light hits an object, it's the object's atomic structure that determines what we see, whether it's translucent marble... clear water... or green leaves.

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:51.040
It all depends on how light interacts with the atoms.

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:58.960
If we can manipulate those atoms, then we can ultimately control what the world looks like.

00:08:58.280 --> 00:09:12.360
This is exactly what David Smith and his team in North Carolina are beginning to do, by creating artificial materials called meta-materials.

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:27.880
We started thinking about interesting things we could do with artificial materials, and one thing that came up was, we could make something like an invisibility cloak.

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:42.320
What we've done is to take materials that are commonly found, something like copper and plastic, which is what our circuit board materials are made from, and we've placed patterns in the copper so that they're tiny circuits.

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:51.440
And these circuits act as artificial atoms in a regular material, except now we've made an artificial material.

00:09:51.880 --> 00:10:05.000
And this altered material has a unique property - it can bend electromagnetic radiation around itself, making it invisible to microwaves.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:10.360
The lime green waves are microwaves.

00:10:09.400 --> 00:10:15.960
When an ordinary copper ring is placed in their path, the waves are disturbed.

00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:26.200
But when the meta-material is placed there, the waves bend around it and seamlessly merge on the other side, as if there was nothing there.

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:29.320
An invisibility cloak is something entirely new.

00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:43.760
It's a structure that doesn't exist in nature, something that couldn't be fashioned out of existing materials and something that really functions in an almost science fictiony way, that you might imagine wouldn't have been possible just a few years ago.

00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:47.640
We've demonstrated the principle of invisibility at microwave frequencies.

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:54.440
Microwave frequencies are a few centimetres in size, or at least, maybe the size of your thumb.

00:10:54.080 --> 00:11:02.560
So now we're looking into the future, and whether or not we can do this invisible light.

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:11.720
In addition to microwaves, scientists have already succeeded in bending red and blue light.

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:17.480
Full invisibility may be just decades away.

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:23.880
The first applications are likely to be for military stealth.

00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:26.480
But it's hard to imagine we'll stop there.

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:33.720
Oh, my Lord!

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:44.680
The ancients have always been fascinated by the property of invisibility.

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:48.640
Over 2,000 years ago Plato refers to this story.

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:56.920
Once there was a poor shepherd who finds a cave, and inside the cave there's a ring, a ring of invisibility.

00:11:56.320 --> 00:12:03.480
And he uses that ring to sneak into the king's castle, seduce the queen, plot against the king.

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:07.040
He killed the king and became the next monarch.

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:17.720
Well, Plato used that story to show that invisibility is so powerful, it could cause societies to disintegrate.

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:20.720
Well, I'm not sure about that.

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:30.760
But I'm sure about one thing, and that is, our mastery over the properties of matter will massively open up our horizons.

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:35.440
In the past, new materials often had a profound impact on our society.

00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:46.160
Concrete and steel, first mass produced in the 19th century, reshaped our cities, and the lives of all city dwellers.

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:59.720
The Brooklyn Bridge was the largest steel suspension bridge ever built, its massive weight held up by 3,600 miles of hefty steel cable.

00:12:58.760 --> 00:13:09.920
When it opened in 1883, it allowed commuters into Manhattan and changed the city forever.

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:18.080
But I believe the impact of steel will be nothing compared with the new materials we'll be creating in the future.

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:27.480
In the 21st century science is experimenting with new classes of materials like carbon resins, ceramics and polymers.

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:42.520
And one of the most promising is a substance that's actually stronger and lighter than steel, and in fact, you could replace the steel in these cables with fibres as thin as a human hair.

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:50.120
That's the promise of carbon nanotubes.

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:53.120
Carbon nanotubes are a miracle of nature.

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:59.040
They're made out of individual carbon atoms arranged in a hollow cylinder.

00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:02.200
The cylinder surface is just one atom across.

00:14:01.400 --> 00:14:04.240
The diameter is only 50 atoms across.

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:08.920
And these tubes can be billions of atoms long.

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:13.120
These extraordinary dimensions give carbon nanotubes their unique properties.

00:14:12.400 --> 00:14:21.600
Their atoms are bonded with the strength of diamonds, yet they have the flexibility of fibre.

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:31.360
Here at MIT, Stephen Steiner and John Hart are going to show me how to grow my own.

00:14:30.680 --> 00:14:32.000
So this is where it all happens?

00:14:31.040 --> 00:14:37.160
These are our furnaces where we grow nanotubes.

00:14:36.200 --> 00:14:40.120
So tell me, where does the carbon come from?

00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:40.560
It comes from a gas, which is in this tank.

00:14:39.760 --> 00:14:56.840
It's actually an age-old process, where you take a carbon-containing gas, and you put your chip on which we want to grow nanotubes, heat up the furnace and the heat causes the gas to decompose, and by that chemical reaction we can grow billions of nanotubes.

00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:59.880
It's not dangerous, is it?

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:07.560
No, we just don't want to get the samples dirty, because the process needs to be pretty clean, as we're growing such small things.

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:18.000
Our substrate has catalyst seeds, nano particles of a metal, and these act as the seeds from which the nanotubes grow.

00:15:17.240 --> 00:15:23.240
We're going to pre-heat them for the growth process.

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:32.880
It's hard to believe that under this tiny piece of glass we're creating one of the strongest materials known to man.

00:15:32.280 --> 00:15:44.320
Yet carbon nanotubes are so small, we can only see them under a powerful microscope.

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:48.320
This is a block of nanotubes, and that's a human hair.

00:15:47.360 --> 00:15:53.600
So now we can focus in... and there we have a strand of carbon nanotubes sitting on the human hair.

00:15:52.760 --> 00:16:00.280
We can zoom in and compare the size of the hair to the size of the nanotubes.

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:07.000
Even this strand contains hundreds, thousands of nanotubes altogether.

00:16:06.200 --> 00:16:12.320
It really puts into into perspective how small the nanoscale really is.

00:16:11.360 --> 00:16:16.360
So far we can only grow short lengths of carbon nanotubes.

00:16:15.480 --> 00:16:25.680
But hundreds of businesses and researchers are racing to develop longer carbon nanotubes in order to harness their huge potential.

00:16:24.840 --> 00:16:39.920
In the future we might be able to use carbon nanotubes for un-smashable cars, un-collapsible buildings, ultra-light jet planes.

00:16:38.960 --> 00:16:49.720
And some people even believe we could use them to build a highway into space.

00:16:49.560 --> 00:17:04.760
This is the famous Seattle Space Needle, built in 1962 for the World's Fair, at the dawn of the space age, when people dreamed about visiting Mars and Venus.

00:17:03.800 --> 00:17:05.960
Well, that never happened.

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:09.520
50 years later, the space programme seems to be stuck.

00:17:08.560 --> 00:17:11.520
Space travel is simply too expensive.

00:17:12.040 --> 00:17:16.480
We need a way to go into outer space without expending all that expensive rocket fuel.

00:17:15.599 --> 00:17:22.640
Well, one thing out of science fiction is the space elevator.

00:17:21.839 --> 00:17:26.440
But it was considered just a far-fetched curiosity... until recently.

00:17:25.599 --> 00:17:33.280
With the amazing strength and lightness of carbon nanotubes, it's now a serious proposition.

00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:37.920
Space elevators are a very clever idea.

00:17:37.120 --> 00:17:40.920
And there's no reason why they shouldn't work.

00:17:40.640 --> 00:17:51.560
The idea that you just, essentially, lower a carbon nanotube rope down onto the surface and winch things up...

00:17:50.840 --> 00:18:03.800
It sounds like science fiction, but in my opinion, anything that's scientifically possible, and not ruled out by the laws of physics, should be possible, in engineering terms, at some point in the future.

00:18:03.240 --> 00:18:19.440
The space elevator was the brainchild of a visionary Russian scientist, Konstantin Tsiolkovsky, who was inspired way back in 1895 by the newly-built Eiffel Tower.

00:18:18.680 --> 00:18:26.880
Since the 1970s, NASA has been funding a trickle of research into what is essentially a permanent lift into space.

00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:36.400
A space elevator is nothing but a super-strong cable up to 60,000 miles in length, suspended from outer space and anchored on the planet Earth.

00:18:35.960 --> 00:18:40.440
What keeps it afloat is the spin of the Earth.

00:18:39.520 --> 00:18:43.200
Think of a ball on a string.

00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:50.800
As you twirl a ball on a string, it doesn't fall down, because of the centrifugal force of being spun in a circle.

00:18:49.840 --> 00:18:56.240
Now, you can calculate that steel is not strong enough to resist this centrifugal force.

00:18:56.080 --> 00:19:08.360
But for the first time in history we have a substance, carbon nanotubes, with more than enough strength to resist the pressure of being suspended from outer space.

00:19:07.760 --> 00:19:22.800
And that's why NASA is offering a half-million dollar prize to the first group that can build a simple prototype of a space elevator.

00:19:21.960 --> 00:19:31.320
Just outside Seattle, one of the most promising teams is using laser power to drive a robot climber up a cable.

00:19:30.720 --> 00:19:32.560
How do you do?

00:19:32.520 --> 00:19:35.800
Can you...?

00:19:34.840 --> 00:19:36.960
We need another cook in the kitchen.

00:19:36.080 --> 00:19:37.440
Yeah.

00:19:39.480 --> 00:19:51.520
The team, called Lasermotive, hope their system will overcome one of the biggest problems of space travel.

00:19:51.600 --> 00:20:06.120
50 years into the space age, and we're still stuck with the fundamental stumbling block, that, when a rocket blasts off, we have this enormous booster rocket lighting up the sky, only just for a few minutes.

00:20:05.360 --> 00:20:18.040
90% of the rocket fuel, 90% of the hardware goes into overcoming just the first few hundred miles of the gravity field of the planet Earth.

00:20:17.160 --> 00:20:24.080
The space elevator allows you to leapfrog past that gravity barrier.

00:20:23.200 --> 00:20:35.480
It uses no rocket fuel whatsoever, has minimal hardware, and would drive down the cost of space travel by a factor of perhaps 100.

00:20:34.720 --> 00:20:37.200
Three...two...one...

00:20:50.040 --> 00:20:58.680
For the first time, space could be open to all.

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:13.440
With its promise of cheap and easy access, the space elevator could allow us to mine space minerals, or beam solar energy back to Earth.

00:21:12.680 --> 00:21:20.200
Or it could simply give us a different perspective on our planet.

00:21:19.800 --> 00:21:26.920
Visionaries have always dreamed about the colonisation of outer space.

00:21:26.760 --> 00:21:33.880
The space elevator could be the key step toward eventually reaching the stars.

00:21:33.880 --> 00:21:40.840
And it might have another, deeper impact.

00:21:40.440 --> 00:21:47.040
Our horizons often determine our understanding of just who we are.

00:21:46.160 --> 00:21:56.080
For most of human history, for example, our horizons were determined by our tribe, which numbered just a few hundred individuals.

00:21:55.240 --> 00:22:04.280
But gradually it expanded to include large city states, large nation states and industrial empires.

00:22:03.400 --> 00:22:13.880
Well, in the future, when access to outer space is for everyone, not just for the rich, then our horizons will expand to include the entire planet.

00:22:13.600 --> 00:22:25.240
And we'll move away from seeing ourselves divided by religion, ethnicity or nationality.

00:22:24.560 --> 00:22:39.080
When we look down from outer space at the planet Earth, not only will we realise how precious life is, but the fact that we belong in a truly planetary civilisation.

00:22:38.440 --> 00:22:48.320
At that point, our consciousness, our horizons, will be truly planetary.

00:22:47.840 --> 00:23:01.000
But as we move toward a planetary society, there's one crucial issue we'll need to master - energy.

00:23:00.200 --> 00:23:12.040
When astrophysicists scan the galaxy for evidence of other civilisations, they categorise them according to their energy signatures.

00:23:11.320 --> 00:23:20.200
Energy is necessary for survival, for development, and helps to define a civilisation's capabilities.

00:23:19.360 --> 00:23:22.480
And there are three types of these civilisations.

00:23:21.600 --> 00:23:29.080
A type one civilisation controls all planetary sources of energy, they control the weather.

00:23:28.240 --> 00:23:33.320
They control the oceans and the hurricanes.

00:23:32.440 --> 00:23:46.360
A type two civilisation has exhausted all planetary sources of energy, and they get their energy by consuming the output of their mother star.

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:50.200
A type three civilisation is galactic.

00:23:49.520 --> 00:23:59.600
They use the energy of tens of billions of star systems, and they roam the galactic space lanes with their starships.

00:23:58.760 --> 00:24:02.560
Why are there three types of civilisations?

00:24:01.640 --> 00:24:11.800
Because there are three types of energy sources in the galaxy - planets, stars and the galaxy itself.

00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:17.080
So where does that leave us?

00:24:16.240 --> 00:24:20.880
We are a type zero civilisation.

00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:25.240
We are so primitive we don't even control the energy resources of our planet.

00:24:24.520 --> 00:24:30.720
We get our energy from dead plants - oil and coal.

00:24:34.200 --> 00:24:46.360
Our progress as a civilisation depends on whether we're able to move beyond our dependence on fossil fuels.

00:24:45.520 --> 00:24:54.520
A few years ago I lived through one of the greatest blackouts in the history of the United States.

00:24:53.600 --> 00:24:56.760
In the Northeast we had a total blackout.

00:24:55.840 --> 00:24:58.160
The effects were immediate.

00:24:57.360 --> 00:25:03.400
First of all, the subway lines were paralysed, transportation came to a grinding halt.

00:25:02.440 --> 00:25:09.760
Second of all, commerce came to a halt because you couldn't use your credit card in machines any more.

00:25:09.200 --> 00:25:15.200
Not only that, food began to rot because refrigerators demanded electricity.

00:25:14.360 --> 00:25:22.600
And if you lived in a 50-floor skyscraper, you had to walk 50 flights just to get home.

00:25:21.720 --> 00:25:30.840
So without electricity, it's like living in a pre-industrial society.

00:25:33.240 --> 00:25:45.240
The New York blackout was in 2003, but today, climate change is forcing us to question our huge appetite for energy.

00:25:44.480 --> 00:25:54.280
But can we really imagine reducing our energy usage?

00:25:54.400 --> 00:26:07.560
Everything that is good about modern civilisation, clean water, medicine, extending life expectancy, everything that we do requires energy.

00:26:07.040 --> 00:26:15.360
So to me, energy conservation is almost a... it's not a desirable goal, right?

00:26:14.520 --> 00:26:20.360
Conserving energy is necessary if you generate it in a dangerous way.

00:26:19.480 --> 00:26:22.840
And we do generate it in a dangerous way at the moment.

00:26:22.080 --> 00:26:30.880
But I see no reason, from a scientific perspective, why that has to continue.

00:26:30.120 --> 00:26:41.040
Energy, especially fossil fuels, is so scarce that it provokes conflicts between nations.

00:26:40.240 --> 00:26:46.360
So imagine an energy source which is safe, clean, efficient and abundant.

00:26:46.200 --> 00:26:48.920
It could have a profound effect on society.

00:26:47.960 --> 00:26:50.240
It could change everything.

00:26:49.960 --> 00:26:56.640
And that energy source could be nuclear fusion.

00:26:55.840 --> 00:26:59.600
Our sun is a massive fusion reactor.

00:26:58.920 --> 00:27:08.880
Its core is so hot that atoms of hydrogen are forced together until they fuse, giving off massive amounts of energy.

00:27:07.920 --> 00:27:23.040
Our mastery over matter could give us cheap energy forever, because the fuel that sustains fusion is abundant on Earth - water.

00:27:22.400 --> 00:27:35.520
The biggest challenge is how to contain a plasma of hydrogen atoms that's so hot, it will incinerate anything it touches.

00:27:34.920 --> 00:27:43.360
At the world's largest experimental fusion reactor in Oxfordshire, that's what they do, every day.

00:27:42.840 --> 00:27:47.000
..Eight, seven, six, five, four, three, two, one... ..zero.

00:27:57.880 --> 00:28:13.360
Marie-Line Mayoral is a French physicist who has dedicated her life to mastering fusion energy.

00:28:12.400 --> 00:28:24.920
When I was a child, they built a big nuclear power plant near my house, and I was extremely afraid that something will, I don't know, explode, or... !

00:28:24.200 --> 00:28:27.920
And then I talked to my parents that, er...

00:28:27.040 --> 00:28:33.880
I will work in a nuclear power plant, in order to make it safer.

00:28:33.040 --> 00:28:39.240
Well, at the end, I don't work in a nuclear power plant, but...

00:28:38.440 --> 00:28:45.200
I'm working on the project to replace them by something safer.

00:28:44.800 --> 00:28:53.080
To contain the plasma, scientists have been refining a design called the Tokamak reactor.

00:28:52.240 --> 00:29:01.600
It's basically a magnetic bottle, and the magnetic fields hold the fiery plasma away from the edges.

00:29:00.760 --> 00:29:09.960
We can reach, without any trouble, 50 million degrees, 100 million degrees.

00:29:09.200 --> 00:29:11.640
This is not now a big deal to do that.

00:29:10.680 --> 00:29:15.040
The point is, to do that for a certain time.

00:29:14.240 --> 00:29:23.600
At this experimental fusion reactor, Mayoral and her colleagues can control the plasma for 30-second bursts.

00:29:23.200 --> 00:29:30.480
It's impressive, but it isn't long enough to produce any usable amounts of energy.

00:29:29.640 --> 00:29:43.600
What scientists need is a bigger reactor, that will allow them to keep the plasma going long enough to reach the critical point at which fusion becomes self-sustaining.

00:29:42.800 --> 00:29:48.000
The science of nuclear fusion has been around for half a century.

00:29:47.520 --> 00:29:50.680
In principle, we know we can do it.

00:29:49.960 --> 00:30:00.400
But as a society, we've lacked the commitment to allocate enough resources to solve all the many technical challenges.

00:29:59.880 --> 00:30:09.280
But as scientists grapple with the hottest plasma on Earth, the political climate is beginning to change.

00:30:08.360 --> 00:30:19.080
Last year, governments representing half the population of the world decided to fund a new fusion reactor.

00:30:18.640 --> 00:30:27.800
The International Thermonuclear Experimental Reactor, to be built in France.

00:30:27.240 --> 00:30:36.640
It's designed to be big enough to create self-sustaining fusion energy for the first time.

00:30:36.040 --> 00:30:41.080
Fusion energy may finally become a reality.

00:30:40.120 --> 00:30:48.600
Between 15 and 20 years from now we will be ready to start building a real power station which will produce electricity.

00:30:47.880 --> 00:30:51.360
That could be operating within 30 years, a few prototypes.

00:30:51.000 --> 00:30:53.920
After that, by the...

00:30:53.160 --> 00:31:03.760
Before the middle of the century, we hope to have large-scale fusion power.

00:31:03.200 --> 00:31:09.720
Fusion, er, for me, it's a way of producing electricity in a safe way and in a cheap way.

00:31:09.720 --> 00:31:13.040
And and we have to try that, basically.

00:31:12.120 --> 00:31:15.760
There is no other way.

00:31:17.000 --> 00:31:23.880
If we had energy that's safe, clean, cheap and unlimited, imagine what we could do.

00:31:24.400 --> 00:31:30.560
We could control global warming.

00:31:29.840 --> 00:31:35.120
We could replace fossil fuels and oil.

00:31:34.240 --> 00:31:36.480
Feed an expanding population.

00:31:35.880 --> 00:31:40.000
Meet the needs of a growing world.

00:31:39.040 --> 00:31:48.080
Ultimately, the quantum revolution will give us a whole range of sustainable, renewable energy sources.

00:31:48.080 --> 00:31:56.360
And it will also give us a new technology, whose impact could be even greater - nanotechnology.

00:31:55.520 --> 00:32:03.640
Nanotechnology will allow us to redesign the world by building with atoms, one by one.

00:32:02.880 --> 00:32:11.600
When you can do that, you're talking about controlling the fundamentals of matter and energy.

00:32:11.920 --> 00:32:16.120
And that's a breathtaking moment.

00:32:15.240 --> 00:32:22.120
You know, that's a thing where you're no longer STUDYING nature in science, you're CREATING nature, you're creating new realities.

00:32:21.480 --> 00:32:24.520
A nanometre is a billionth of a metre.

00:32:24.080 --> 00:32:27.120
You can't see it.

00:32:26.200 --> 00:32:30.000
In fact, it's about ten atoms across.

00:32:29.280 --> 00:32:35.320
Now imagine a machine that is on the scale of a nanometre.

00:32:34.880 --> 00:32:40.520
A machine that's made out of a few hundred, a few thousand individual atoms.

00:32:39.640 --> 00:32:43.240
Well, you may say to yourself, "That's science fiction.

00:32:42.440 --> 00:32:47.520
"How can you make a saw, a lever, a wheel "made our of maybe a thousand atoms?"

00:32:46.600 --> 00:32:50.000
Well, Mother Nature has already done it.

00:32:49.040 --> 00:32:56.000
Mother Nature can take raw materials, and out of that, create life.

00:32:55.240 --> 00:33:10.040
The goal of nanotechnology is to create nano machines on the scale of living cells, like proteins, DNA or bacteria, and design them to perform equally complex tasks.

00:33:09.200 --> 00:33:16.360
These miniature mechanical devices could turn toxic waste into harmless matter.

00:33:16.120 --> 00:33:25.200
Or they could travel through our blood vessels to mend cells from within.

00:33:24.800 --> 00:33:34.960
The first step toward building these nano machines is to hijack living systems at the molecular level, and engineer them to do what we want.

00:33:34.240 --> 00:33:42.360
At MIT, Andreas Mershin is doing this with plant proteins.

00:33:42.560 --> 00:33:48.200
We're trying to use plants to create solar power.

00:33:47.440 --> 00:33:53.240
Plants have developed this amazing ability to capture sunlight and create and store chemical energy.

00:33:52.480 --> 00:34:14.639
We can grab the machine, the protein inside the plant, photosystem one which is responsible for generating this energy for the plant and hijack its function, put it on a substrate of our choosing and use the energy to create solar electrical power, and to run light bulbs or any kind of machinery that you want.

00:34:13.760 --> 00:34:28.960
Mershin modifies the plant protein, then connects it to a bed of specially engineered nano wires that act as electrodes.

00:34:29.040 --> 00:34:34.960
The final product will be a nano machine that delivers solar power in the form of a paint.

00:34:34.159 --> 00:34:36.679
Our goal is to provide an alternative to regular silicon-based solar panels.

00:34:35.719 --> 00:34:44.719
They're big and heavy and they're not ideal to be transported to a remote village in Africa or China or India.

00:34:43.840 --> 00:34:57.400
What we're trying to do is produce a material that's almost like a paint that you can paint on a metallic surface, expose it to light and have yourself some electricity.

00:34:56.440 --> 00:35:06.320
Hijacking nature to make nano machines offers an awesome new power with endless potential.

00:35:05.360 --> 00:35:23.680
We're domesticating protein molecules, protein living machines, to do things that are interesting to us, and there's a huge menagerie of such proteins in the natural world, from plants and animals and all sorts of other things, such as bacteria, that can make, pretty much, anything you want.

00:35:23.160 --> 00:35:32.000
The trick is to be able to engineer them and change them, and harness their power and make them do what you want them to do.

00:35:32.560 --> 00:35:43.520
One of the most exciting applications of these hybrid machines is to cure illness from within our own bodies.

00:35:42.640 --> 00:35:59.560
Here in this very estuary in Rhode Island, scientists have found bacteria which they are using to power a prototype nano robot that could fit in our blood vessels.

00:35:58.600 --> 00:36:00.800
Hi, how are you?

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:02.640
Nice meeting you.

00:36:02.800 --> 00:36:04.040
Under the microscope we have thousands of bacteria.

00:36:03.120 --> 00:36:08.400
And here you have a joystick...

00:36:07.600 --> 00:36:08.840
I see.

00:36:08.120 --> 00:36:12.880
..And then you can control - left, right, go forward and backward, by pressing those buttons over here.

00:36:13.760 --> 00:36:19.520
Well, I see thousands of bacteria... and I can move them down...

00:36:19.400 --> 00:36:22.800
Yes, I can see them move down...

00:36:22.440 --> 00:36:26.840
Up...

00:36:25.920 --> 00:36:28.840
Moving to the right...

00:36:28.640 --> 00:36:38.400
'These bacteria are sensitive to magnetic fields, 'so I'm actually controlling them with magnets.'

00:36:37.440 --> 00:36:38.840
Moving to the left...

00:36:37.880 --> 00:36:41.440
I almost feel omnipotent.

00:36:40.560 --> 00:36:48.920
Like a god controlling thousands and thousands of bacteria.

00:36:48.120 --> 00:36:50.560
Amazing.

00:36:49.840 --> 00:36:57.480
Martel's team has already used the magnetic field of an MRI machine to drive a tiny device along a living artery.

00:36:56.560 --> 00:37:16.360
The plan is then to shrink this device, place the bacteria inside it, and use their rotating tails as miniature engines to drive it around our bodies and deliver drugs Our main goal is to develop new medical tools.

00:37:15.600 --> 00:37:24.160
And the main application we have right now is to target the tumour inside the human body.

00:37:23.320 --> 00:37:41.320
So the trick here is to be able to... integrate those tiny motors into... a machine that could deliver drugs, and be able to control it by computer.

00:37:40.480 --> 00:37:42.040
You can barely see that thing.

00:37:41.160 --> 00:37:42.280
Yeah, exactly.

00:37:41.320 --> 00:37:47.360
So the next step is to engineer a microchip to give the nano machine intelligence.

00:37:46.560 --> 00:37:49.600
So this microchip actually is huge for us.

00:37:49.000 --> 00:37:51.880
It's too big.

00:37:51.080 --> 00:37:57.120
You want to get it so small you could fit it into the capillaries of the body?

00:37:56.280 --> 00:37:57.920
Exactly.

00:37:57.120 --> 00:37:59.240
So they can go anywhere they want.

00:37:58.400 --> 00:38:05.880
Well, here what we see is one of those kind of robots.

00:38:05.560 --> 00:38:08.600
This version have six motors.

00:38:07.640 --> 00:38:17.160
Six motors that contain several bacteria which are controlled, because you need to be able to turn left and right, and the same time to go forward or backward.

00:38:16.320 --> 00:38:20.000
How many bacteria are pushing this chip?

00:38:19.960 --> 00:38:23.080
About 80 bacteria.

00:38:22.200 --> 00:38:28.120
So this is like a chip with propellers, right?

00:38:27.400 --> 00:38:29.160
Exactly, exactly.

00:38:28.520 --> 00:38:40.120
In the future, swarms of invisible nano robots might be permanently patrolling our blood systems, repairing tissue and keeping us healthy.

00:38:40.440 --> 00:38:48.880
When I think about the implications of the work being done here, I can't help but think about my father and my mother.

00:38:48.040 --> 00:38:55.520
My father died of Alzheimer's disease, and now it afflicts my mother.

00:38:54.960 --> 00:38:59.240
It's a horrible disease.

00:38:58.440 --> 00:39:00.560
It robs you of your very sense of who you are.

00:38:59.760 --> 00:39:22.000
And yet, I can now imagine a time when we can use these kinds of targeted therapies and artificial intelligence and nanotechnology to zero in on the brain, to clear out the brain of the proteins that have gummed up our neural circuits, and give us hope again.

00:39:21.280 --> 00:39:32.760
Imagine what we can do with this.

00:39:31.880 --> 00:39:51.960
You could imagine injecting millions of them into your bloodstream, and they would first serve as watchdogs, waiting for the early onset of cancer, or some other terrible disease and allowing you to intervene far earlier than you ever could before.

00:39:51.320 --> 00:40:05.560
The next generation of nanobots are expected to be the hunter-killer ones, and those are the ones who you could send in and destroy cancer cells right at the beginning.

00:40:04.720 --> 00:40:19.320
And it's even theoretically possible to imagine that these could go after fat cells, so that you'd never see an obese human being again.

00:40:18.400 --> 00:40:28.600
But nanotechnology is not only being developed for health and longevity.

00:40:29.760 --> 00:40:37.720
Well, on the battlefield the nanobots are going to do a lot of things.

00:40:37.040 --> 00:40:39.520
For instance, they can seek and destroy specific targets.

00:40:38.720 --> 00:40:54.760
You can make them very, very smart so they can identify not only geographical areas but specific weapons systems or communications systems that you wish to attack.

00:40:53.920 --> 00:40:58.920
You've heard about, you know, the surgeons that you can inject into your bloodstream.

00:40:58.040 --> 00:41:11.600
Well, the surgeon can go in there to repair a clogged blood vessel, or they might be able to go in there and punch holes in blood vessels, if you want to destroy an adversary.

00:41:10.880 --> 00:41:15.400
The embryonic stages are here today, and a lot of work is being done.

00:41:15.720 --> 00:41:24.040
There are national-level programmes in several countries in the world developing these technologies.

00:41:24.160 --> 00:41:28.920
They will, you know, in the period of a couple of decades, you're going to see major changes.

00:41:28.520 --> 00:41:42.520
What is particularly exciting and extremely controversial about nanotechnology is the belief that nano machines will be designed to scavenge molecules from their environment to reproduce themselves.

00:41:41.920 --> 00:41:55.280
They will be smart and autonomous, but just like the viruses and bacteria they're modelled on, they will have the potential to do enormous damage.

00:41:54.560 --> 00:42:02.560
In nanotechnology, people are all talking about creating man-made objects that can produce more of themselves.

00:42:02.000 --> 00:42:06.760
They're self-replicating.

00:42:06.000 --> 00:42:16.280
And you've got to be real careful about a self-replicating anything, because you'd better know where the off switch is.

00:42:16.000 --> 00:42:32.520
In the grey goo scenario, this is a world in which these nanobots might keep on replicating until they consume all of the energy on the entire planet, leaving nothing but grey goo behind.

00:42:31.880 --> 00:42:43.160
Prince Charles' concern over grey goo brought the threat of nanotechnology to the public's attention.

00:42:42.320 --> 00:43:01.880
But, for some, nanotechnology malfunctioning is not the The much bigger risk seems to be, not a freak accident, but the deliberate design of weapons systems using molecular nanotechnology.

00:43:01.600 --> 00:43:15.480
And with this advanced form of nanotech, it would be possible to build different kinds of weapons systems for which it's very difficult to see how an effective defence would be possible.

00:43:14.960 --> 00:43:32.840
In my view, the advanced form of nanotechnology is arguably the greatest existential risk that humanity is likely to confront in this century.

00:43:32.200 --> 00:43:36.520
Oh, you can be guaranteed it will eventually get into the wrong hands.

00:43:36.440 --> 00:43:42.120
The best we can do is both safeguard against that eventuality, but it will happen, and then be one step ahead.

00:43:41.240 --> 00:43:49.920
For every move there's a counter move, and we need to continue to research in these areas.

00:43:49.080 --> 00:44:00.720
I believe we'll build safeguards to destroy or deactivate nanobots if they get out of control, or into the wrong hands.

00:43:59.800 --> 00:44:07.960
And I think the potential benefits of nanotechnology vastly outweigh the perceived risks.

00:44:07.840 --> 00:44:19.640
Nano machines could give us the amazing ability to disassemble molecules into individual atoms and reassemble them into new structures.

00:44:18.840 --> 00:44:31.800
This means we could make anything we want at home using a personal fabricator, a box full of nano machinery that builds objects from the atom up.

00:44:32.320 --> 00:44:41.040
Commercial companies are already promoting a future of personal fabrication.

00:44:41.840 --> 00:44:54.000
'Future advances in molecular nanotechnology 'will enable desktop appliances to manufacture products 'far better than today's best.

00:44:54.880 --> 00:45:01.880
'Each product is built from beneath, layer by layer, 'by billions of tiny machines all working together.'

00:45:02.120 --> 00:45:08.720
As I see it, a personal fabricator is a factory on a desktop.

00:45:07.840 --> 00:45:26.760
It'll be about the size of a microwave oven, or maybe a vending machine, but instead of dispensing candy bars or soda pop, it creates pretty much anything you can imagine, from high-tech items like PCs and mobile phones, all the way down to basic necessities like food, clothing and medicines.

00:45:25.920 --> 00:45:31.480
Almost anything you can imagine, this device will create.

00:45:32.920 --> 00:45:45.840
Neil Gershenfeld of MIT believes this technology will work in the same way that the body is built, from proteins that produce other proteins.

00:45:44.920 --> 00:45:50.840
Your body has a protein that assembles other proteins like building blocks and it really runs a program.

00:45:50.880 --> 00:46:03.240
So the research that we're doing is, we're just on the edge of learning mathematically, technically, how to programme materials, how to fundamentally digitise fabrication.

00:46:02.680 --> 00:46:08.440
So if you can manipulate atoms to modify proteins, you're in a sense improving on nature?

00:46:07.600 --> 00:46:18.680
The research road map is leading, 20 years from now, to make the Star Trek replicator, the molecular assembler that really builds everything on a molecular scale.

00:46:17.800 --> 00:46:24.080
So that at that stage, it's a lot like life, where a part can make a part that can make a part.

00:46:23.760 --> 00:46:41.800
We're just at the edge now of this very parallel digital revolution in fabrication, and what's it's going to do is take all of the programmability you're used to, in the digital world on your desktop, and bring it to the physical world, so you can really programme the physical world.

00:46:41.000 --> 00:46:42.760
So you're programming reality, in some sense?

00:46:42.560 --> 00:46:44.280
In a very explicit sense.

00:46:44.280 --> 00:47:00.240
So in the future, instead of shopping from our computers or in the high street, we could be producing everything we want and need at home, programming matter with our personal fabricators.

00:46:59.280 --> 00:47:08.520
To inventor Ray Kurzweil, this will have radical implications for society.

00:47:07.720 --> 00:47:16.040
Actually, I think, overcome the have/have-not divide, because we'll be able to create physical products... just from information.

00:47:15.240 --> 00:47:34.000
So a shirt, a meal or a computer, we'll be able to essentially print out using these nanotechnology table-top fabricators, and really, very easily and very inexpensively, meet the material needs of any size biological population.

00:47:33.280 --> 00:47:41.640
But with nanotechnology and personal fabrication, perhaps, at least, the simple basic necessities, can be gotten simply by asking for it.

00:47:40.880 --> 00:47:45.720
So possessions are not going to mean that much.

00:47:44.880 --> 00:47:45.960
What are your thoughts?

00:47:45.240 --> 00:47:47.360
I agree.

00:47:46.560 --> 00:47:50.680
I mean, it's not just the necessities but, er...

00:47:49.840 --> 00:47:51.440
We will still have wealth.

00:47:50.640 --> 00:47:59.920
There'll still be wealthy people, there'll still be proprietary forms of information you have to pay for, not everything is going to be free.

00:47:59.120 --> 00:48:17.560
But the lowest level, you'll be able to live very comfortably with adequate housing and food and clothing and fashion, and really living, you know, what we today consider almost a wealthy lifestyle at the lowest level.

00:48:17.240 --> 00:48:28.160
If a self assembler ever does become possible, that's going to be one of history's great "Holy shit!"

00:48:27.440 --> 00:48:28.520
Moments.

00:48:28.040 --> 00:48:37.040
Then you're really talking about changing the world into something beyond anything we've ever recognised before.

00:48:36.240 --> 00:48:40.600
Personal fabrication could challenge everything about our society and economy.

00:48:39.720 --> 00:48:46.600
The role of money, the nature of commerce, the gap between rich and poor.

00:48:46.160 --> 00:48:56.320
Some scientists believe that in a mere 20 years we'll have personal fabricators right in our living room.

00:48:55.480 --> 00:48:58.320
I'm not so sure.

00:48:57.480 --> 00:49:01.280
There are a lot of hurdles that still have to be negotiated.

00:49:00.400 --> 00:49:08.960
I would say, perhaps, by mid to late 21st century we'll begin to see the outlines of personal fabrication.

00:49:08.000 --> 00:49:18.480
We're talking about the ultimate Holy Grail of nanotechnology, the ability to create something out of almost nothing.

00:49:20.560 --> 00:49:34.640
The coming revolution, the revolution that's going to happen when we really learn how to manipulate matter at the molecular level, at the subatomic level, will take everyone, including me, by surprise.

00:49:33.920 --> 00:49:38.040
Because it really removes the barrier to doing, pretty much, anything you want.

00:49:37.480 --> 00:49:44.360
Anything that you can see around you, including yourself, that's solid, is only a pattern.

00:49:44.120 --> 00:49:48.880
It's a pattern of atoms and molecules.

00:49:47.960 --> 00:49:56.200
And if you know how to manipulate those patterns, then, really, the possibilities are absolutely endless.

00:49:55.320 --> 00:50:03.720
The quantum revolution radically challenges the way we view the world.

00:50:02.840 --> 00:50:08.120
It's already transformed some of the ideas from science fiction, and turned them into science fact.

00:50:07.920 --> 00:50:17.080
Here in Vienna, one of the most fantastic concepts of science fiction has already been turned into reality.

00:50:16.960 --> 00:50:24.320
And in fact, it may represent our ultimate mastery over matter itself.

00:50:23.600 --> 00:50:27.960
It's called teleportation.

00:50:27.120 --> 00:50:37.040
In my group, we have achieved teleportation ten years ago, and we are doing it on a regular basis, all the time improving any new things and so on.

00:50:36.400 --> 00:50:39.560
So it's a simple fact.

00:50:38.960 --> 00:50:42.600
Teleportation exists.

00:50:43.680 --> 00:50:52.880
This is the world's first teleportation machine.

00:50:51.920 --> 00:51:10.480
With it, Professor Anton Zeilinger and his team are teleporting individual particles of light, called photons, from one prism to another.

00:51:17.840 --> 00:51:24.240
And it can happen over any distance.

00:51:26.440 --> 00:51:33.400
The present record about distance in teleportation is 600 metres.

00:51:32.520 --> 00:51:39.480
This was done by our group from one side of the River Danube over to the other side.

00:51:38.640 --> 00:51:41.880
But there are no limits.

00:51:41.160 --> 00:51:48.280
We might do it some day to the Moon or to Mars, or who knows?

00:51:47.480 --> 00:51:53.680
Professor Zeilinger is inducing a peculiar state in his particles of light.

00:51:53.160 --> 00:51:58.360
It's called quantum entanglement.

00:51:57.440 --> 00:52:00.520
It's a mysterious bond between a pair of photons.

00:51:59.600 --> 00:52:04.960
When you change one, the other changes instantly.

00:52:04.040 --> 00:52:12.600
So the photon itself isn't teleported, just the information it contains.

00:52:13.120 --> 00:52:19.960
What we really teleport in quantum teleportation is the information carried by a system.

00:52:19.280 --> 00:52:25.840
This information is teleported over to another system which assumes exactly that information, therefore it becomes identical with the original.

00:52:25.040 --> 00:52:37.080
The original loses its properties... and the new one is made up of different matter, it's not the same matter as the original.

00:52:36.240 --> 00:52:46.800
What's interesting is that the original photon's information is lost, which could have huge implications for teleporting anything on a larger scale.

00:52:46.440 --> 00:52:56.240
Is it out of the question, or is it just very, very difficult a cat, or a human?

00:52:55.720 --> 00:52:59.200
Well, we can dream.

00:52:58.400 --> 00:53:07.640
And if you dream about teleportation of humans, then all kind of questions arise, like, "What does it mean to be me?"

00:53:06.680 --> 00:53:18.480
When someone teleports me and I know that what is being teleported is information, not matter, not the stuff I'm made of... who is it who ends up over there?

00:53:17.560 --> 00:53:27.800
How does the average person react to the fact that you have to be destroyed in Suppose we put an ad in the newspaper, just for fun.

00:53:27.160 --> 00:53:39.360
Just for fun we put an ad in the newspaper which said that we want to try teleportation of people and we look for volunteers, but we cannot guarantee that it will be successful, 100%.

00:53:38.480 --> 00:53:41.880
Maybe only 10 or 20%.

00:53:41.200 --> 00:53:47.240
I bet many people would show up and want to be the first one teleported!

00:53:50.640 --> 00:53:57.440
In the next few years, scientists will teleport the first simple molecule.

00:53:56.520 --> 00:54:02.240
Then complex molecules, perhaps the first virus within a decade or so.

00:54:01.600 --> 00:54:13.320
Teleporting a human may seem impossibly advanced, but science is changing our world faster than it has ever done in human history.

00:54:12.480 --> 00:54:27.800
If you'd have told me 20 years ago that my mobile phone would be able to pinpoint my position, using a network of satellites, to within a couple of metres, then I would have almost certainly not believed you.

00:54:27.960 --> 00:54:40.160
But now the global positioning system is in everybody's car, it's appearing in everybody's mobile phones and in watches.

00:54:39.400 --> 00:54:48.000
We are witnessing an unparalleled explosion in scientific knowledge.

00:54:47.160 --> 00:54:51.360
We've learned more in the past 50 years than in all of human history.

00:54:51.800 --> 00:54:55.720
And why is that?

00:54:55.240 --> 00:55:05.320
It's because of the synergy, the intense interplay between the three great revolutions: the quantum, computer, and biotech revolutions.

00:55:04.560 --> 00:55:12.920
And when these revolutions work in harmony they feed off each other and unleash a tidal wave of scientific discovery.

00:55:12.120 --> 00:55:24.360
And this synergy will only accelerate into the 21st century giving us technologies that we can only dream about.

00:55:24.480 --> 00:55:33.120
One of the best examples of the impact of this powerful synergy is the Human Genome Project.

00:55:32.520 --> 00:55:50.360
Back in 1985, when people were talking about sequencing the human genome, most sensible people thought, first of all, it was probably impossible, and that if it were possible, it would take till the year 2015 and cost the earth.

00:55:49.600 --> 00:55:51.520
Well, of course it happened much faster than that.

00:55:51.240 --> 00:55:56.720
In the year 2000, for only a few billion.

00:55:55.800 --> 00:55:58.120
Chump change, as these things go(!)

00:55:57.440 --> 00:56:11.440
And the reason is that the price of the computer kept on dropping and dropping and dropping, and that's what people who were projecting the future hadn't taken into consideration.

00:56:12.440 --> 00:56:15.440
There are scores of examples of the synergy between these three great revolutions.

00:56:14.560 --> 00:56:27.240
For example, we now use laser beams to scan hundreds of genes at any one given time, and then we use supercomputers to process this vast amount of genetic information.

00:56:26.520 --> 00:56:35.720
But it's not only genes, we're using supercomputers to reveal the secrets of everything, from proteins to the human brain.

00:56:34.880 --> 00:56:40.480
Synergy is truly leading us toward mastery of matter and life.

00:56:39.640 --> 00:56:45.400
Basically, the rate of technical progress and its impact is doubling every decade.

00:56:46.560 --> 00:56:49.840
And exponential growth is quite phenomenal.

00:56:49.040 --> 00:57:00.080
It literally means that these technologies, which are already very influential, will be a billion times more powerful than they are today 25 years from now.

00:56:59.200 --> 00:57:01.640
And that's really quite transformative.

00:57:03.600 --> 00:57:14.480
We're beginning to be able to take control of the workings of our minds, our memories, our metabolisms, our personalities and our kids.

00:57:13.560 --> 00:57:26.440
We have the means, through genetics, robotics, information and nanotechnology, to control matter, energy and life itself at the most basic levels.

00:57:25.680 --> 00:57:38.360
We've never seen anything like this before, and this is raising profound questions about what it means to be human and what our lives, our futures and our children will be like.

00:57:39.080 --> 00:57:51.680
The intense cross-pollination between the computer, biotech and the quantum revolutions will give us unprecedented power in the 21st century.

00:57:50.720 --> 00:57:59.080
The power to create a planetary civilisation, a civilisation based on progress, scientific knowledge and trust.

00:57:58.560 --> 00:58:03.880
Or, on the other hand, the power to unleash unparalleled chaos and anarchy on the Earth.

00:58:03.400 --> 00:58:10.480
The choice is ours, and the time to discuss these questions is now.

00:58:09.640 --> 00:58:17.760
That's because the current generation holds the future of the Earth in its hands.
